entry chain-reaction
term: Chain Reaction
difficulty: intermediate
image: ../../assets/images/terms/chain reaction.webp
related_terms: nuclear-fission
related_terms: critical-mass
related_terms: nuclear-reactor
related_terms: control-rods
related_terms: neutron
quick_facts: Each fission releases 2–3 neutrons
quick_facts: Critical (k = 1): steady power · supercritical: exponential growth
quick_facts: Delayed neutrons give reactors their controllability
quick_facts: Control rods tune the chain by absorbing neutrons
quick_facts: Requires enough fissile material and the right neutron energy

sources:
    {"organization":"World Nuclear","title":"Physics of Nuclear Energy","url":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/nuclear-fuel-cycle/introduction/physics-of-nuclear-energy.aspx"}

definition:
    A self-sustaining series of nuclear fission events: each fission splits a heavy nucleus and releases 2–3 fresh neutrons, which can go on to split further nuclei. If exactly one fission per generation goes on to cause another (k = 1), the chain is critical and steady — that's a reactor. More than one (k > 1) grows exponentially — that's a bomb or a supercritical accident. Reactors stay controllable thanks to delayed neutrons, which act as a slow-moving fraction of the chain.

debunking_snippet:
    “A reactor can explode like an atomic bomb.” It can't — reactor fuel is far too dilute in fissile material (a few % U-235 vs >90% for weapons). Chernobyl's explosion was steam and chemical energy, never a nuclear blast.
